INT. FIELD BAKERY IN A RUINED CATHEDRAL - NIGHT, 1944

Ovens burn where the altar once stood. Stained glass lies in colored shards along the nave. Flour sacks form a low wall. Distant artillery thumps like a slow heart.

SERGEANT ELIAS MWANGI, forties, sleeves rolled, kneads dough on a pew. CORPORAL TADEUSZ BIELSKI, twenties, sits against a pillar rubbing his boot. PRIVATE LACHLAN MUIR, nineteen, feeds wood into an oven. LIEUTENANT HARUKO TANAKA, thirties, a quartermaster with a notebook, stands at the door reading a list by candlelight.

He lifts the dough, slaps it down. She turns a page. The private fans the fire with his cap.

                    HARUKO
          Eleven hundred loaves by dawn. Sergeant Mwangi, will that be possible?

                    ELIAS
          Possible, ma'am. Pleasant, no.

                    TADEUSZ
          Sergeant, the flour is damp.

                    ELIAS
          Flour is always damp in this country. Everything is.

                    LACHLAN
          Sergeant, do you think the cathedral minds?

                    ELIAS
          Minds what?

                    LACHLAN
          Us. Baking in it.

                    ELIAS
          Private, it's the warmest room for fifty miles. If God wanted it empty He should've kept the roof on.

                    TADEUSZ
          My grandmother would have crossed herself.

                    HARUKO
          Mine would have asked for the recipe.

                    LACHLAN
          What did she bake?

                    HARUKO
          Rice cakes. Very patiently. She said bread was for people in a hurry.

                    ELIAS
          She was right.

                    TADEUSZ
          Then what are we doing?

                    ELIAS
          Hurrying. Badly.

A shell lands somewhere far off. Dust sifts from the vault. Nobody looks up.

                    LACHLAN
          Lieutenant, do you ever write home?

                    HARUKO
          Every Sunday.

                    LACHLAN
          What do you say?

                    HARUKO
          That the bread is excellent.

                    ELIAS
          It is.

                    TADEUSZ
          It is not. It is edible.

                    ELIAS
          Edible is excellent, Corporal. Ask anyone who's been hungry.

Tadeusz nods slowly. Lachlan adds a log. The fire pops. Haruko tears out a page, folds it, and slips it into her pocket.

                    HARUKO
          Gentlemen, what time is it?

                    ELIAS
          Nearly three.

                    HARUKO
          Then you have two hours to make a thousand loaves.

                    ELIAS
          And what will you do, ma'am?

                    HARUKO
          Pray the bread is excellent.
